{1040}{37l A History of Grayhills}

// Modern Farming Techniques
// A book about Crop Rotation. Can be given to farmer for quest. Farmer needs some way of improving production. Only given if PC ha a certain intelligence (12). Copy quest from ‘Fallout 1 - Shady Sands farmer’, where PC can tell farmer about crop rotation, except this time, PC gives farmer book.
{1050}{37c 

Moderne Farming Techniques

An Experiment in 
Sustainable Farming Practices

By George Washington


}
{1051}{37l After many years of experimentation, I believe I have devised methods with which one can ensure the long term viability of one's farm. One of these methods, crop rotation, I believe has the most beneficial advantage to the moderne farmer.


--- Crop Rotation ---

After much examination, I have discovered that growing the same crop year after year in the same field results in a reduction of quality for the crop grown. There appears to be little one can do about this. However, by changing the crop grown in a field after harvest, to say, another crop of significant difference, then I find that the reduction in quality does not occur. 

For instance, the experimentation of one of my own fields involved the following rotation of planting:

Seven Year Rotation Cycle

Year 1: Corn and Potatoes 
Year 2: Wheat 
Year 3: Buckwheat
Year 4: Wheat 
Year 5: Clover or Grass 
Year 6: Clover or Grass 
Year 7: Clover or Grass 


--- How Crop Rotation Works ---
I change my crops year by year in a pre-planned sequence to ensure that no crop is grown in the same field two years running. It appears that currently, most plantation owners are depleting their soil by overplanting. 

My method of crop rotation is designed to improve the long-term productivity of the land. By rotating crops, I believe that fields do not become 'exhausted' or the soil depleted of nourishment. By coupling this method with the use of fertilizers, I am able to increase the life of the field far beyond what it may have been. In my plans, I have emphasized restoring the soil as much as restoring production. 
}
{1052}{37l In my above example, you can see that in the first year, I have planted corn and potatoes. The second year I planted wheat, followed in the third by buckwheat for green manure. In the fourth year, I again planted wheat, followed by three successive plantings of grass or clover. While the land restored itself during these plantings of grass, I used the pasture to fatten livestock. I then repeat the process for another seven year cycle. No crop, except for grasses, should be planted in the same field two years in a row.


--- How Crop Rotation Helps ---
I find that rotation reduces the needs for fertilizer. This is because some crops replace the nutrients that corn and other grain crops remove. Also, pesticide costs may be reduced by naturally breaking the cycles of weeds, insects and diseases. Grass and legumes in a rotation protect water quality by preventing excess nutrients from entering water supplies and meadow or small grains cut soil erosion dramatically.
}
{1053}{37l It is important to note that the crops planted must be suited to the farmers soils. It is imperative that the farmer design crop rotations to meet the nutrient needs of his soil for later crops. For crop rotations which include hay the rotation can be lengthened by maintaining the existing hay stand for additional years.


--- Planting Groups ---
My own trials have revealed that it is not necessary to take note of every individual crop that is planted in one's field. Instead, it is only necessary to group the plants into categories. For convenience, I have listed the categories below.

ROOTS - Potatoes, Carrots, Beetroot, Jerusalem artichokes, Parsnips

LEGUMES - Peas, Runner beans, Broad beans, French beans

BRASSICAS - Cabbages, Cauliflowers, Brussels sprouts, Broccoli, Turnips, Radishes 

OTHERS - Lettuces, Onions, Celery, Cucumber, Spinach, Leeks, Sweetcorn, Tomatoes, Marrow 

PERMANENT - Rhubarb, Globe artichokes, Asparagus, Herbs, Fruit
}
{1054}{37l 
In my learnings, I have found that Brassicas should always be kept together as a group. One must never plant brassicas in the same ground two years running. Potatoes should also be kept together every year. If the potatoes are taking up a lot of space, try moving all the other 'roots' into the 'others' group. Note also that some 'roots' are manure-friendly (such as potatoes) while others are not (such as parsnips). One could split the 'others' group to plant individual crops where one has some space. It is possible for one to have a three-year crop rotation such as Roots, Brassicas and then Others. However, one must not go any lower than the minimum of a three year cycle.


--- Fertilizers  ---

I have experimented with many manures in an attempt to improve the quality of my soil. Jethro Tull, a leading agriculturalist and associate of mine, believes that plants have tiny mouths on their roots which eat the foods in the soil. Tull and I have observed that plants grow better in loose soil, rather than heavy, compacted soil. Tull's theory is an attempt to explain why this is so. He therefore believes that perhaps loose soil fits better into the plants mouths. As such, loose soil appears to be the key to better crops. It is possible to loosen ones' soil through the use of farm implements, or tools, such as plows and harrows, or by the use of manure and other such fertilizers.

Through constant experimentation, I have concluded that cultivation and soil amendments are both essential to better harvests. 

Manure :
The organic content of animal manure improves the soil and its nutrients make it a natural fertilizer. I have built a special manure storage facility or 'stercorary'. Manure ferments there until it is ready to be spread on the fields. I have also begun the process of fencing my pasture animals on grass fields to provide manure in the fields and to fatten my livestock.
}
{1055}{37l Creek Mud :
I find that using the dark mud from creeks, which is high in organic content, has benefits similar to manure. 

Fish Heads : 
These provide nutrients and organic material as they decompose in the soil.

Marl :
Marl is a chalky clay substance. Spreading it over ones' fields creates a more favorable soil environment for many crops. 

Plaster of Paris :
Plaster of Paris is chiefly made of gypsum. Gypsum can be used as a soil amendment that loosens heavy, clay like soils, without changing the natural balance of the soil. Gypsum also adds other important elements for plant growth. 

Green Manure :
Buckwheat, clover, and peas are plowed under by oneself to replenish the soil. This process of plowing under green manures helps improve the productivity of ones' soil

Ashes :
Ashes can be used to help improve the soil and prevent bugs from ruining the crops. Seeds mixed with ashes and sand are also easier to spread over ones fields. 

}
{1056}{37l By combining these two methods of crop rotation and fertilizers, it is possible to increase the nutrient life of ones' fields by many years.
}

